Project/domain documentation belongs here; the central platform owns only reusable development process.

## First checks

```bash
python3 scripts/platform_doctor.py
python3 scripts/agent_doctor.py
```

`platform_doctor.py` checks the installed platform contract and OpenSpec compatibility. `agent_doctor.py` checks current GitHub/Git readiness for starting or finishing work.

## Working on a change

For non-trivial product or architecture work, create/update the OpenSpec change first. If implementation changes direction, update the relevant OpenSpec artifact before code knowingly diverges.

Start through the shared profile-aware entrypoint:

```bash
python3 scripts/start_task.py my-change --task "OpenSpec my-change: task range" --scope "path/or/module"
```

Run selected checks before completion:

```bash
python3 scripts/select_checks.py
python3 scripts/select_checks.py --execute
```

For a non-trivial OpenSpec change, completion is not just “all tasks checked”. The agent must run `/opsx:verify`, resolve material findings, record `OpenSpec-Verify: PASS` in the change's `verification.md`, and archive through:

```bash
python3 scripts/openspec_lifecycle.py archive my-change
```

Only after archive is committed should the task be published through:

```bash
python3 scripts/finish_task.py
```

`finish_task.py` enforces OpenSpec lifecycle hygiene, re-fetches `origin/{{ main_branch }}` and either creates a PR or performs a guarded direct push according to `.dev-platform.toml`. It never force-pushes or auto-resolves divergence. A completed task list left active blocks publication instead of relying on a reminder.

Platform-managed CI is self-contained in this repository and updates only through reviewed Copier releases; it does not execute `dev-platform@main` or require private cross-repository workflow access.

See `AGENTS.md` and `docs/engineering/`.
